{
  "slug": "shortest-distance-from-all-buildings",
  "title": "Shortest Distance from All Buildings",
  "topic": "graphs",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "breadth-first search from every building with accumulated distance sums",
  "statement": "You are given a rectangular grid of integers. A `0` is empty land, a `1` is a building and a `2` is an obstacle that nobody can walk through. You want to pick one empty cell and build a house there. The house must be able to reach every building by walking up, down, left or right, where empty land and buildings may both be walked through and obstacles block the way. The cost of a cell is the sum of the walking distances from that cell to every building, and a cell only counts as a candidate when every building is reachable from it. Return the smallest cost among the candidate cells, or `-1` when there is no candidate cell at all (in particular when the grid contains no building, and when the grid contains no empty cell).\n\nFor example the grid `[[1, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 1]]` has two buildings, and every empty cell reaches both of them; the cheapest one is the centre or any of the four edge middles, with cost `4`.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= grid.length <= 20`, `1 <= grid[i].length <= 20` and `0 <= grid[i][j] <= 2`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "grid = [[0]]",
      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "grid = [[0, 0, 2], [2, 0, 2], [0, 2, 2]]",
      "output": "-1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(grid: number[][])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Walking from a house to a building takes exactly the same number of steps as walking from that building to the house, so the direction of the search is yours to choose.",
    "If you search from the buildings instead of from the houses, one traversal tells you the distance to every cell at once; add those distances into a shared total grid.",
    "Keep a second grid counting how many buildings reached each cell. Only cells whose counter equals the number of buildings are candidates, and the answer is the smallest total among them."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force takes every empty cell in turn, runs a breadth-first search from that cell over the walkable cells (empty land and buildings, with obstacles blocked), and then reads the distance recorded at each building. The cell is a candidate only when no building came out unreached, and its cost is the sum of those distances; one full BFS per empty cell gives O((m*n)^2). The optimal solution walks in the opposite direction: it runs one breadth-first search from each building and accumulates, for every cell it reaches, a running total of distances and a counter of how many buildings reached it. After all buildings have been processed a cell is a candidate exactly when its counter equals the number of buildings, and its accumulated total is already its cost, so the answer is the smallest such total.",
    "why_optimal": "The definition adds up one distance for every (cell, building) pair, and B has to be multiplied into the work because the grid is fixed and the buildings are far apart in general, so any solution that realizes every pair costs at least O(m*n*B); running exactly B traversals over the m*n cells reaches that bound. The reversal is what makes it cheap: the distance from a house to a building equals the distance from that building to the house, so one traversal per building covers all cells at once instead of one traversal per cell.",
    "pitfalls": "Reporting a building as a house, since buildings are reachable from themselves but are not empty cells. Returning `0` when the grid has no building; with nothing to reach there is no candidate cell and the answer is `-1`. Comparing candidate validity against a distance that merely exists instead of checking that the reached counter equals the building count, which wrongly accepts cells cut off from some building by obstacles. Blocking movement on buildings instead of only on obstacles. Forgetting to add a building's own distance to the totals, so the sum is off by one per building. Reading the grid dimensions from row `0` when the grid could be empty."
  },
